window.docContents[92].resolve({"/Maps/#section-claw-free-graphs":{"contents":"We say that H contains an induced copy of G if there is an embedding G ↪g H.\nThis is written G ⊴ H.\n\n#source IsIndContained -- def IsIndContained (G : SimpleGraph V)\n                       --       (H : SimpleGraph W) : Prop := Nonempty (G ↪g H)\n\n\nThere isn't currently a standard name for the property of not containing an induced\ncopy of a graph in Mathlib, so we introduce one here (following the pattern of\nFree).\n\nA.IndFree B means that B does not contain an induced copy of A./-- A.IndFree B means that B does not contain an induced copy of A. -/\nabbrev IndFree {V W : Type*} (A : SimpleGraph V) (B : SimpleGraph W) := ¬A ⊴ B\n\n\nThe claw is the complete bipartite graph K[1, 3].\n\nA graph is claw-free if it does not contain an induced copy of K[1, 3]./-- A graph is *claw-free* if it does not contain an *induced* copy of K[1, 3]. -/\nabbrev ClawFree {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) : Prop := K[1, 3].IndFree G\n\n\nNote that claw-free refers to induced copies of the claw. A copy of the claw that\nneed not be induced is just a vertex with three distinct neighbours, so being\nclaw-free in that sense would simply mean that every vertex has at most two\nneighbours. The two notions really are different: K[4] is claw-free, since any four\nof its vertices induce a copy of K[4], yet K[1, 3] ⊑ K[4], since every vertex of\nK[4] has three neighbours (see the exercises).\n\nLine graphs are always claw-free.\n\ntheorem clawFree_lineGraph {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) : L[G].ClawFree := by\n  classical\n  -- Suppose not: then we have an embedding f : K[1, 3] ↪g L[G].\n  rintro ⟨f⟩\n  -- The centre of the claw is adjacent to each leaf, so its edge f (.inl 0) shares\n  -- a vertex w i with each leaf edge f (.inr i).\n  have key : ∀ i, ∃ v, v ∈ (f (.inl 0) : Sym2 V) ∧ v ∈ (f (.inr i) : Sym2 V) := by\n    intro i\n    have hadj : K[1, 3].Adj (.inl 0) (.inr i) := by simp\n    rw [← f.map_adj_iff, lineGraph_adj_iff_exists] at hadj\n    exact hadj.2\n  -- The choose tactic turns key into a function w : Fin 3 → V together with\n  -- hwc : ∀ i, w i ∈ f (.inl 0) and hwl : ∀ i, w i ∈ f (.inr i).\n  -- This uses the axiom of choice.\n  choose w hwc hwl using key\n  -- The centre edge is s(a, b) with a ≠ b.\n  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := Sym2.mem_iff_exists.1 (hwc 0)\n  let a := w 0\n  have hne : a ≠ b := by\n    have hmem : (f (.inl 0) : Sym2 V) ∈ G.edgeSet := (f (.inl 0)).2\n    rw [hb, mem_edgeSet] at hmem\n    exact hmem.ne\n  -- Each leaf's shared vertex is one of the two endpoints a or b.\n  have hsides : ∀ i, w i = a ∨ w i = b := by grind\n  -- Three leaves, two endpoints, so two leaves i ≠ j must share the same endpoint!\n  -- We do this by the pigeonhole principle, applied to the map Fin 3 → Prop\n  -- sending i to the proposition w i = a. This gives i ≠ j with w i = a and\n  -- w j = a taking the same truth value and hence w i = w j.\n  -- (Since either both are a or both are ≠ a and hence equal b.)\n  obtain ⟨i, j, hij, hsame⟩ :=\n    Fintype.exists_ne_map_eq_of_card_lt (w · = a) (by decide)\n  have hwij : w i = w j := by grind\n  -- But then the two leaf edges share w i, hence are adjacent in the line graph,\n  -- contradicting that leaves of the claw are non-adjacent.\n  have hfne : f (.inr i) ≠ f (.inr j) := fun h => hij (by simpa using f.injective h)\n  have hadj : L[G].Adj (f (.inr i)) (f (.inr j)) :=\n    lineGraph_adj_iff_exists.2 ⟨hfne, w j, hwij ▸ hwl i, hwl j⟩\n  simp [f.map_adj_iff] at hadj\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.8. Claw-Free Graphs*","id":"/Maps/#section-claw-free-graphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-naming-conventions":{"contents":"We have now met a few Mathlib names, and they follow fixed patterns. Knowing the\nrules will help us guess a lemma's name. The full rules can be found in Mathlib's\nnaming conventions.\nThe key points are:\n\n* Theorems are named in snake_case, predicates and structures in UpperCamelCase\n(IsIsolated, Dart) and other definitions in lowerCamelCase (neighborSet).* A theorem's name describes its statement, with the conclusion first and any\nhypotheses after of. Symbols have standard names: ¬ is not, ∈ is mem,\n⊆ is subset, ≠ is ne, ∉ is notMem and ⊤ is top. So\nnot_isDiag_of_mem_edgeSet says that an edge e ∈ G.edgeSet is not a loop, and\nneighborSet_subset_support says that G.neighborSet u ⊆ G.support.* An equivalence (↔) that characterises membership of a set, such as mem_edgeSet\nor mem_support, is named after its left-hand side alone. Other equivalences often\nend in _iff, such as Sym2.mk_isDiag_iff.* A theorem about h : G.Adj u v lives in the namespace SimpleGraph.Adj, so we can\nuse it with dot notation, as with h.ne and h.left_mem_support. A prime marks a\nminor variant, so h.ne proves u ≠ v while h.ne' proves v ≠ u.\n\nWhen the rules don't allow us to guess the name, we can search for it as described in\nFinding Lemmas.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.5. Naming Conventions","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-naming-conventions"},"/Subgraphs/#section-subgraphs-of-mycielskians":{"contents":"As worked examples of Subgraph.map and Subgraph.comap we consider subgraphs of\nthe Mycielskian mμ(G). If H : G.Subgraph then we can map H to a subgraph of\nmμ(G) using the embedding of G into mμ(G) given by\nEmbedding.mycielskianLeft G.\n\nThe subgraph of the Mycielskian mμ(G) corresponding to a subgraph H of G./--\nThe subgraph of the Mycielskian mμ(G) corresponding to a subgraph H of G. -/\n@[simps!]\ndef Subgraph.mycielskianLeft (H : G.Subgraph) : mμ(G).Subgraph :=\n  Subgraph.map (Embedding.mycielskianLeft G).toHom H\n\n\nPulling mycielskianLeft H back along the same embedding recovers H.\n\nlemma Subgraph.comap_mycielskianLeft (H : G.Subgraph) :\n    H.mycielskianLeft.comap (Embedding.mycielskianLeft G).toHom = H := by\n  ext v w <;> simp_all [Relation.Map, H.adj_sub]\n\n\nCombined with the Galois connection Subgraph.map_le_iff_le_comap, this shows that\nmycielskianLeft both preserves and reflects the subgraph order.\n\nlemma Subgraph.mycielskianLeft_le_mycielskianLeft_iff {H₁ H₂ : G.Subgraph} :\n    H₁.mycielskianLeft ≤ H₂.mycielskianLeft ↔ H₁ ≤ H₂ := by\n  rw [Subgraph.mycielskianLeft, Subgraph.map_le_iff_le_comap,\n    Subgraph.comap_mycielskianLeft]\n\nend basics\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Maps and Comaps","header":"9.4.1. Subgraphs of Mycielskians","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-subgraphs-of-mycielskians"}});